// Package fake documents stateful fakes generated by the fake template for [mockery].
//
// The template is the file fake.templ in this directory
// (with its JSON schema fake.templ.schema.json).
// A generated fake replaces a dependency in tests which are clearer as world-state transitions
// than as a list of expected calls:
// it combines default behavior with queued per-call overrides and call history.
// Unexpected calls fail loudly, so a test can't silently pass on zero values.
// The API may change in new versions of the template:
// a project keeps its copy of the template until it updates,
// and breaking changes are visible at compile time.
//
// This package contains the example interface [Store] and its fake generated by the template:
// see [FakeStore], its constructors [NewFakeStore], [NewFakeStoreFrom]
// and per-method types like [FakeStoreGetFunc] and [FakeStoreGetFuncCall]
// for the generated API.
//
// # Naming
//
// All names are derived from mockery's structname option.
// Let S be the structname.
// For an interface with a method M the template generates:
//
//   - the fake struct S with a field MFunc per method;
//   - constructors NewS and NewSFrom;
//   - per-method types SMFunc (hooks and call history) and SMFuncCall (one recorded call).
//
// For example, this .mockery.yml generates a fake FakeRepo
// into the package porttest next to the interface's package port:
//
//	_anchors:
//	  fake: &fake
//	    template: file://.mockery/fake.templ
//	    dir: '{{.InterfaceDirRelative}}/{{.SrcPackageName}}test'
//	    pkgname: '{{.SrcPackageName}}test'
//	    filename: 'fake.{{.InterfaceFile | base}}'
//	    structname: 'Fake{{.InterfaceName | firstUpper}}'
//	packages:
//	  example.com/app/port:
//	    interfaces:
//	      Repo:
//	        config: *fake
//
// Use different structnames for outputs which share a package
// (e.g. a fake in the interface's package and another one in its internal test package),
// otherwise their names clash.
//
// # Semantics
//
// Every method of a fake has a default hook and a FIFO queue of one-shot hooks,
// both kept in the method's field (e.g. [FakeStore.GetFunc]).
// A call takes the hook at the front of the queue and discards it,
// or calls the default hook when the queue is empty.
// When the queue is empty and there is no default hook, the call panics
// with "unexpected invocation of FakeStore.Get".
//
//   - [NewFakeStore] and the zero value of [FakeStore]: there are no default hooks,
//     so a call to a method which was not configured panics.
//   - [NewFakeStoreFrom]: default hooks delegate to the methods of the given implementation.
//     Use it to have a real implementation (e.g. an in-memory one) with some calls overridden.
//   - [FakeStoreGetFunc.SetDefaultHook] and [FakeStoreGetFunc.SetDefaultReturn]
//     replace the default hook.
//     Use them for behavior which must not change during a whole test.
//   - [FakeStoreGetFunc.PushHook] and [FakeStoreGetFunc.PushReturn]
//     append a one-shot hook to the queue.
//     Use them to override a single next call:
//     the following calls fall back to the default hook, or panic if there is none.
//   - [FakeStoreGetFunc.History] returns a copy of all recorded calls in order,
//     each with a field per parameter and result (see "Call records").
//     A variadic argument is recorded as a slice in its field,
//     and [FakeStoreGetFuncCall.Args] flattens it.
//     A call is recorded after its hook returns, so a call which panics is not recorded.
//
// Calls, PushHook, PushReturn and History are safe for concurrent use.
// SetDefaultHook and SetDefaultReturn are not synchronized with calls:
// configure default hooks before the fake is used concurrently.
//
// A fake contains mutexes and must not be copied after first use:
// keep it in a variable or pass a pointer to it (go vet reports copies).
//
// # Call records
//
// A field of a call record has the exported name of the method's parameter or result,
// like [FakeStoreGetFuncCall.Key] and [FakeStoreGetFuncCall.Err] (id becomes ID).
// A parameter or result with no name or a blank one is named by mockery after its type:
// S for string, N for int, B for bool, Err for error, Thing for a.Thing, Bools for ...bool,
// with a number when the name repeats (S, S1).
// mockery renames a name which clashes with an imported package (sync to sync1)
// and adds MoqParam to a name derived from a predeclared type like any:
// these additions are removed, so the fields are Sync and Any.
// Only a name ending in a digit keeps the added number: ipv4 clashing with an import is Ipv41.
// Name the results in the interface to get more meaningful fields.
//
// A name which can't be used gets the prefix Arg for a parameter or Result for a result:
//
//   - Args and Results are methods of a call record, so args becomes ArgArgs;
//   - a name already used by a previous parameter or result gets the prefix,
//     so foo and Foo become Foo and ArgFoo, and parameter size with result Size
//     become Size and ResultSize;
//   - a name which is not exported after upper-casing the first letter gets the prefix,
//     so _x becomes Arg_x.
//
// Renaming a parameter or a result in an interface renames the field,
// so tests using the field stop compiling until they are updated.
// Args and Results always keep the order of parameters and results.
//
// # Not supported
//
//   - An unexported interface generated into another package.
//     Generate the fake into the interface's own package or its internal test package instead.
//   - An alias declaration (type A = I) is not an interface to fake:
//     list the aliased I instead.
//
// # Differences from go-mockgen
//
// The first version of the API is the API of the template
// [github.com/powerman/mockery-templates/go-mockgen] with these changes:
//
//   - A single strict constructor NewS instead of the pair NewMockS and NewStrictMockS:
//     an unexpected call must fail loudly by default instead of returning zero values.
//   - Per-method fields are values instead of pointers:
//     this makes the zero value of a fake work exactly like NewS.
//   - Per-method types are named SMFunc and SMFuncCall by the full structname,
//     without trimming a leading "Mock":
//     no special case in naming and no clashes with go-mockgen output in the same package.
//   - Args and Results return []any instead of []interface{}: this is modern Go.
//   - Fields of a call record are named after parameters and results
//     instead of ArgN and ResultN:
//     a test reads call.Key, not call.Arg1, and can't confuse positions.
//
// [mockery]: https://github.com/vektra/mockery
package fake
